BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:[Cantor Arts] Solid Pictures: Photosculpture and the Making of Mod
 ern Likeness
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261108
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261108
DTSTAMP:20261107T175756Z
UID:stanford_campus-localist-52881097589713-52881097623558@local-events
DESCRIPTION:Before 3D printing\, there was photosculpture. Curated by Patr
 ick R. Crowley\, Associate Curator of European Art\, Solid Pictures is the
  first major exhibition to explore these largely overlooked objects first 
 conceived in 1859 by the twenty-nine-year-old French artist and inventor F
 rançois Willème. Employing projection technologies and mechanical instru
 ments\, Willème and his team of operators translated photographic negativ
 es into solid figures across a variety of media\, aiming to create afforda
 bl\n\nAges: All ages\nSource: https://museum.stanford.edu/exhibitions/soli
 d-pictures\nLast synced: 2026-11-07 09:57 PST
LOCATION:Cantor Arts Center
URL:https://museum.stanford.edu/exhibitions/solid-pictures
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:[Stanford University Libraries] Text Machines: Scarlet Thread of t
 he Digital Order (1883-2026)
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261108
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261108
DTSTAMP:20261107T175756Z
UID:stanford_campus-localist-53251159287291-53251159427740@local-events
DESCRIPTION:Stanford Libraries now holds an 1883 edition of a rare book\,
  Les laboureurs: poème tiré de Jocelyn\, whose pages (text and ornamen
 ts) were entirely woven on a Jacquard loom by the firm of silk merchant Jo
 seph-Alphonse Henry. This exhibition explores the cultural contexts and ma
 terial conditions of Les laboureurs’ production\, and considers parall
 els between technology-driven global change in the late nineteenth century
  and in our current day. The exhibition includes microscope images reveali
 ng the\n\nAges: All ages\nLast synced: 2026-11-07 09:57 PST
LOCATION:Green Library\, Bing Wing
END:VEVENT
